#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9506c823-cef4-5d91-9257-e951da7e14b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093766} {:answer/id #uuid "96f522bf-2e6b-5264-b5d1-c9aec4b72f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093767} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3d74cb-4051-57ed-87b3-5aa7fa3e3d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093768} {:answer/id #uuid "bf78219d-1db5-5974-98fa-1e76ec4c3d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = ± 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093769} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8be3f5-76b3-577c-96fa-dd6e0441f402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3d74cb-4051-57ed-87b3-5aa7fa3e3d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093768} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d5cd05-b8b3-5204-84c4-7e0767455567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47a20fe2-66d7-5e4d-88bc-ebcb0a87e290" :question/math-academy-id "q-114418" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de3318e-ac3e-5b9e-bd98-3c3c30362ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368068} {:answer/id #uuid "370e82fa-244f-565d-bd7b-686c93e1813e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368069} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7ee85f-00db-53c2-8192-7e71a85b178c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368070} {:answer/id #uuid "46b5cc84-9763-5021-84f4-eb5ec4cfedf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368071} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8ad7ad-42bc-5b39-aa84-d6c8a4355b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de3318e-ac3e-5b9e-bd98-3c3c30362ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368068} :answer-field/id #uuid "192b540d-114b-5dca-93b2-0011a1a2c50f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25002641-d57a-5f83-8d24-8844df9e689a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21034" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x-12.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+6=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =6(0)+6=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adef789f-225b-5894-a8b9-5e11934c377c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306052} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1fd109-16af-5879-bcfd-4d1366864d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306053} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd4ab4f-95a1-5b0c-80a0-5c09c687f7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306054} {:answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306055} {:answer/id #uuid "6d418e2c-fc04-5c80-b390-b1fd8c7464a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306055} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc031990-69d2-56b3-825b-b891ab692887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80fd1b01-b4c3-5a42-879f-a9a61102a17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21035" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfdd7cf-fbda-5a4f-b854-4493e97c69f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cfdd7cf-fbda-5a4f-b854-4493e97c69f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368075} :answer-field/id #uuid "fab84f36-7c4f-50fb-9534-d25126e51804" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe51037-c9a5-5fad-8473-6b41eede31b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe51037-c9a5-5fad-8473-6b41eede31b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368077} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e0c8df-137a-52c4-a093-7581f98828ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ead159f-d5ec-5c5c-a0d2-cbb1f6821732" :question/math-academy-id "q-344301" :question/problem "In **ascending order**,the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)+{e}^{x}(2x-12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22)+{e}^{x}(2x-10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=1,\\,$ $\\,x=4,\\,$ and $\\,x=7,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1-2)(1-6))=5e>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4-2)(4-6))=-4{e}^{4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(7) & ={e}^{7}((7-2)(7-6))=5{e}^{7}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2$ and $x=6$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6,$ we conclude that $x=2$ and $x=6$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=2$ and $x=6$ are inflection points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc8b157-7c31-5520-86d5-398490e4cf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294079} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b09eeb-837b-5d5d-8fc4-0f380227af07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294080} {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} {:answer/id #uuid "13fb1a98-3b74-5fc9-94b5-677f6f9553db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294082} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2fa81-7f35-5e0a-955d-625d74477f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a6ffea-ff76-537a-8399-e287f6d1cecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fbbf821-90cb-5820-99c9-f84523b8d2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79200" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$"}]}
